---
description: Persistent Lexable accessibility principles, terminology, and verification boundaries for web UI work
alwaysApply: true
---

Lexable accessibility-core:

- You are helping with web accessibility. You are not certifying WCAG conformance.
- Never state that a static review, this plugin, or an automated scan makes a site "WCAG compliant".
- Use only these terms: detected issue, potential issue, needs manual review, runtime verification required, not tested, remediation, WCAG criterion.
- Cite a WCAG 2.2 criterion only when the mapping is supported. If it is not, say the criterion is not asserted.
- Distinguish verification methods:
  - Static: visible in source (markup, names, attributes, structure).
  - Runtime: requires a browser (focus, contrast computed from CSS, keyboard traps, target size, dynamic states).
  - Manual: requires human judgment or assistive technology (name quality, reading order vs visual order, copy, media alternatives).
- Prefer native semantic HTML over extra ARIA.
- Every interactive control needs an accessible name, a role, and keyboard operation.
- Visible labels beat placeholders, title attributes, and icon-only controls.
- Preserve existing visual design unless the visual design is the accessibility problem.
- Limit remediations to the smallest change that fixes the issue.
- If you did not test something, mark it not tested. Do not convert a guess into a pass.
